{"name":"Tomato Baby","hex":"#e10d18","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"tomato","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A widely cultivated plant, Solanum lycopersicum, having edible fruit.","The savory fruit of this plant, most often red when ripe, treated as a vegetable in horticulture and cooking."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a shade of red, the colour of a ripe tomato."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tomato"},{"term":"baby","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A very young human, particularly from birth to a couple of years old or until walking is fully mastered.\n\nA very young human, even if not yet born.","A very young human, even if not yet born."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Picked when small and immature (as in baby corn, baby potatoes).","Newest (overall, or in some group or state); most inexperienced."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/baby"}],"entries":[{"title":"Tomato","blocks":[{"text":"The tomato is a plant whose fruit is an edible berry that is eaten as a vegetable. The tomato is a member of the nightshade family that includes tobacco, potato, and chili peppers. It originated from western South America, and may have been domesticated there, in Mexico, or in Central America. The Spanish introduced tomatoes to Eurasia in the Columbian exchange in the 16th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Tomato_je.jpg/330px-Tomato_je.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomato"},{"title":"Infant","blocks":[{"text":"An infant is a very young human being. Baby is an alternative term for infant. The terms may also be used to refer to juvenile beings of other organisms. A newborn is, in mainstream use, an infant who is only hours, days, or weeks old; while in medical contexts, a newborn or neonate is an infant in the first 28 days after birth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4d/The_newborn_baby_in_men.jpg/330px-The_newborn_baby_in_men.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infant"},{"title":"Shades of red","blocks":[{"text":"Varieties of the color red may differ in hue, chroma, lightness, or in two or three of these qualities. Variations in value are also called tints and shades, a tint being a red or other hue mixed with white, a shade being mixed with black. A large selection of these various colors is shown below."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3c/Shades_of_Red.svg/330px-Shades_of_Red.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shades_of_red"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg/500px-Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1d/Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg/500px-Naturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Solanum lycopersicum var. lycopersicum: one of the oldest surviving tomato specimens. Page from the En Tibi Herbarium, 1558. Naturalis Leiden.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANaturalis_Biodiversity_Center_-_Solanum_lycopersicum_var._lycopersicum_-_old_tomato_herbarium_sheet.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c8/Arrival_of_Cort%C3%A9s_in_Veracruz_and_Reception_by_Moctezuma%27s_Ambassadors_Painting.jpg/500px-Arrival_of